(* The package bala2-base: the two motors and wheel encoders of the base of
   the M5Stack BALA2, an STM32 at I2C address 0x3A on the bus Wire. Grades:
   time-interval-rational, in milliseconds.

   SetMotors (left, right) sets the drives of the motors, each an integer
   from -1023 to 1023, a drive outside that range being a run-time error;
   bala2_drive, of bala2_base_drive.tpe, is the drive of a float, rounded and
   clamped. ReadEncoders () reads the counts of the encoders of the wheels,
   each an integer of 32 bits that increases in the direction in which a
   positive drive turns its wheel; ResetEncoders () sets them to zero.

   The base stops the motors when no SetMotors call has come for the setpoint
   window, 200 ms unless the build flag BALA2_WATCHDOG_MS sets it, and in the
   safe state of the device.

   The bounds of the running times are measured on an M5Stack Gray core on
   the base, with the bus at 400 kHz, by the profile of the device
   (2026-10-14): the lower bound is the least time measured, rounded down,
   and the upper bound 1.5 times the greatest, rounded up. SetMotors, a write
   of 6 bytes, took 0.220 to 0.292 ms, and ReadEncoders, a read of 8 bytes,
   0.462 to 0.653 ms. ResetEncoders, a write of 10 bytes, was measured once,
   0.321 ms; its bounds are those of SetMotors with the transfer of 4 more
   bytes, 22.5 microseconds each at 400 kHz: 0.310 to 0.382 ms. The default
   implementations take a time within the bounds and read zeros.

   On the profile m5stack-gray-radio, an M5Stack Gray with its Wi-Fi radio
   running on core 0, the tasks of the application on core 1 and the link
   encrypted, the profile of the device (2026-10-19) measured SetMotors at
   0.260 to 0.498 ms, ReadEncoders at 0.450 to 0.929 ms and ResetEncoders at
   0.524 to 0.789 ms; earlier runs measured ReadEncoders at 0.431 ms at
   least. Their bounds there have the upper bound 1.5 times the greatest
   time measured with the radio, rounded up, and the lower bound the least
   time measured with or without it, rounded down: SetMotors [0.2, 0.75],
   ReadEncoders [0.4, 1.4] and ResetEncoders [0.3, 1.2]. *)

operation SetMotors : int * int ~> unit # [0.2, 0.45]
default SetMotors _ = delay 1/5

operation ReadEncoders : unit ~> int * int # [0.45, 1]
default ReadEncoders () = delay 1/2; (0i, 0i)

operation ResetEncoders : unit ~> unit # [0.3, 0.6]
default ResetEncoders () = delay 7/20
